tern \"C\" fn(*const c_char, usize, *mut i32) -> *const c_char,\nutf8_check_string: unsafe extern \"C\" fn(*const c_char, usize) -> c_int,\n```\nNote `c_char` is `i8` on this platform, so convert bytes with `b as i8` or `b as c_char`.\n\nFor `utf8_iterate`, the C returns a pointer INTO the input buffer, or NULL, or\n(when `bufsize==0`) the input pointer unchanged. Because the two libraries are\ngiven the SAME buffer, compare the returned pointer as an OFFSET from the buffer\nstart (`ptr as usize - buf.as_ptr() as usize`), and compare NULL-ness first.\n\nWrite these tests, covering CONFIGS.md rows 1-5 and ERRORS.md rows 212-228:\n\n1. `utf8_check_first_all_256_bytes` — every value 0..=255. (CONFIGS 1; ERRORS 214,215,216)\n2. `utf8_encode_boundaries` — codepoints: -2147483648, -1000, -1, 0, 1, 0x7F, 0x80,\n   0x7FF, 0x800, 0xD7FF, 0xD800, 0xDFFF, 0xE000, 0xFFFF, 0x10000, 0x10FFFE, 0x10FFFF,\n   0x110000, 0x7FFFFFFF. For each: fill a 8-byte buffer with 0xAA sentinel bytes in\n   BOTH libs, call, then compare the return code, the whole 8-byte buffer, and the\n   `*size` out-param (initialise it to a sentinel like `usize::MAX` so you also detect\n   \"not written\"). (CONFIGS 3; ERRORS 212,213)\n3. `utf8_encode_randomized` — 20000 random i32 values from `Rng::new(0xU1)`\n   (`rng.next_u64() as i32`), same comparison as above.\n4. `utf8_check_full_sizes_and_randomized` — for `size` in 0,1,2,3,4,5,6 and for\n   2000 random 8-byte buffers per size (from a seeded `Rng`), compare the return\n   value AND the `*codepoint` out-param (initialise to a sentinel -12345 so you detect\n   \"not written\"). Also run each case a second time with `codepoint = null_mut()`\n   to cover the NULL branch. (CONFIGS 2; ERRORS 217,218,219,220,221)\n5. `utf8_check_full_handcrafted` — explicit byte sequences: valid 2/3/4-byte encodings,\n   overlong (C0 80, E0 80 80, F0 80 80 80), surrogates (ED A0 80, ED BF BF),\n   above range (F4 90 80 80, F7 BF BF BF), bad continuation (C2 41, E2 82 41).\n6. `utf8_iterate_all_shapes` — for each of: valid 1/2/3/4-byte sequences, invalid lead\n   bytes, truncated sequences, and 3000 random buffers: try `bufsize` = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4\n   and the exact length. Compare NULL-ness, the returned offset, and `*codepoint`\n   (sentinel-initialised). Also cover `codepoint = null_mut()`.\n   (CONFIGS 4; ERRORS 222,223,224,225)\n7. `utf8_check_string_shapes` — len 0, len 1; random ASCII of len 1..64; random bytes\n   of len 1..64 (5000 iterations); valid random UTF-8 from `Rng::utf8_string`;\n   valid UTF-8 truncated by 1, 2 and 3 bytes at the end; buffers with an embedded NUL;\n   a `len` SHORTER than the real buffer; a `len` of 0 with a non-empty buffer.\n   (CONFIGS 5; ERRORS 226,227,228)\n\nIMPORTANT RULES\n- Never call Rust functions directly; only through `d.rs.<name>` function pointers.\n- Use a FIXED seed for every `Rng` so runs are reproducible.\n- Do NOT modify anything under `c_src/`, and do NOT modify `tests/common/mod.rs`\n  or any other existing file. Create ONLY `tests/t01_utf.rs`.\n- The C is ALWAYS right. If C and Rust diverge, do NOT change the test to hide it —\n  report the divergence precisely in your final message (function, inputs, C value,\n  Rust value).\n\nVERIFY YOUR WORK before finishing:\n```\ncd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-hjHD91/translated_rust\ncargo build 2>&1 | tail -5\ntimeout 600 cargo test --test t01_utf 2>&1 | tail -40\n```\nIterate until it COMPILES and all tests either pass or reveal a genuine C/Rust\ndivergence. Keep total runtime under ~60 seconds (reduce iteration counts if needed,\nbut keep them at least in the thousands).\n\nReport: the exact file path created, the number of tests, the pass/fail counts from\nthe final `cargo test` run, and a precise list of any C-vs-Rust divergences found."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":523,"cache_read_input_tokens":165438,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5