uffer_pop:          unsafe extern \"C\" fn(*mut strbuffer_t) -> c_char,\njsonp_free:             unsafe extern \"C\" fn(*mut c_void),\njsonp_malloc:           unsafe extern \"C\" fn(usize) -> *mut c_void,\n```\nand\n```rust\n#[repr(C)] pub struct strbuffer_t { pub value: *mut c_char, pub length: usize, pub size: usize }\nstrbuffer_t::zeroed()\n```\n`c_char` is `i8` here. IMPORTANT: a buffer allocated by library X must be freed with\nX's `jsonp_free` (each `.so` has its own allocator hooks), and `strbuffer_close`\nmust be called on the same library that ran `strbuffer_init`.\n\nThe observable state after every operation is the triple\n`(sb.length, sb.size, bytes at sb.value)`. Write a helper that snapshots it, e.g.\n```rust\nfn snap(l: &Lib, sb: &strbuffer_t) -> (usize, usize, Vec<u8>) { ... }\n```\nreading `sb.length + 1` bytes from `sb.value` (the buffer is always NUL-terminated)\nand handling `sb.value == null`. Compare C vs Rust snapshots with `eq(...)`.\n\nWrite these tests, covering CONFIGS.md rows 6-11 and ERRORS.md rows 207-211:\n\n1. `init_and_close_state` — `strbuffer_init` on a zeroed struct in both libs; compare\n   return value, `length`, `size` (must be 16) and `value[0]` (must be 0). Then\n   `strbuffer_close` and compare `value` (NULL), `length` (0), `size` (0).\n   (CONFIGS 6)\n2. `append_byte_growth_boundary` — from a fresh buffer, append bytes one at a time and\n   snapshot after EVERY append, for a total of 40 appends (crosses the 16→32→64 growth\n   boundaries; note the C grows when `size >= strbuff->size - strbuff->length`).\n   Include byte values 0x00, 0x7F, 0x80 (negative `c_char`), 0xFF. (CONFIGS 7)\n3. `append_bytes_sizes` — for `size` in 0,1,2,15,16,17,31,32,100,1000,10000: fresh\n   buffer, one `strbuffer_append_bytes` of that many bytes, snapshot. Then a second\n   append of the same size, snapshot again. Use deterministic content from a seeded\n   `Rng` and include content with embedded NUL bytes. (CONFIGS 8)\n4. `append_bytes_randomize