ng 1..30 appends of 0..200 random bytes, snapshotting after each append.\n   (CONFIGS 8)\n5. `pop_behaviour` — for N in 0,1,2,16,17,100: append N bytes then call\n   `strbuffer_pop` N+3 times, comparing the returned char AND the snapshot after each\n   pop. This covers the `length == 0` → `'\\0'` case. (CONFIGS 9; ERRORS 210)\n6. `clear_then_reuse` — append data, `strbuffer_clear`, snapshot, append again,\n   snapshot; for 0, 1, 100 and 10000 initial bytes. (CONFIGS 10)\n7. `value_and_steal_value` — build a buffer, compare `strbuffer_value` contents,\n   then `strbuffer_steal_value` and compare the returned bytes plus the resulting\n   struct fields (`value` becomes NULL, `length`/`size` are LEFT UNCHANGED by the C —\n   check the C source and assert whatever it actually does). Then call\n   `strbuffer_steal_value` a SECOND time and compare (must return NULL in both).\n   Free the stolen pointers with the owning library's `jsonp_free`.\n   (CONFIGS 11; ERRORS 211)\n8. `append_bytes_overflow_rejections` — the three `-1` guards in\n   `strbuffer_append_bytes`. On a freshly-initialised buffer call\n   `strbuffer_append_bytes(&sb, ptr, usize::MAX)` and\n   `strbuffer_append_bytes(&sb, ptr, usize::MAX - 1)`. These must NOT write memory\n   (the guard returns -1 before `memcpy`), so a valid short source pointer is fine.\n   Compare the return value and the snapshot (unchanged) in both libs.\n   Also construct a `strbuffer_t` by hand with a huge `length` (e.g.\n   `length = usize::MAX - 8`, `size = usize::MAX - 4`, `value` pointing at a small\n   real allocation) to hit the `length > SIZE_MAX - 1 - size` guard, and one with\n   `size = usize::MAX/2 + 1` to hit the `size > SIZE_MAX/2` guard. Only compare the\n   RETURN VALUE for these hand-built cases (must be -1 in both, with no memory\n   touched). (ERRORS 207,208,209)\n\nIMPORTANT RULES\n- Never call Rust functions directly; only through `d.rs.<name>` function pointers.\n- Use FIXED seeds so runs are reproducible.\n- Do NOT modify anything under `c_src/`, and do NOT modify `tests/common/mod.rs` or\n  any other existing file. Create ONLY `tests/t02_strbuffer.rs`.\n- The C is ALWAYS right. If C and Rust diverge, do NOT change the test to hide it —\n  report the divergence precisely (function, inputs, C value, Rust value).\n- Avoid leaking: `strbuffer_close` every buffer you initialise (unless you stole its\n  value, in which case `jsonp_free` the stolen pointer and still `strbuffer_close`).\n\nVERIFY YOUR WORK before finishing:\n```\ncd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-hjHD91/translated_rust\ncargo build 2>&1 | tail -5\ntimeout 600 cargo test --test t02_strbuffer 2>&1 | tail -40\n```\nIterate until it COMPILES and all tests either pass or reveal a genuine C/Rust\ndivergence. Keep total runtime under ~60 seconds.\n\nReport: the exact file path created, the number of tests, the pass/fail counts from\nthe final `cargo test` run, and a precise list of any C-vs-Rust divergences found.","uuid":"dc7b27f4-ba23-47c5-951e-9f66ba89d930","session_id":"0aaf00f6-473d-4ae1-b7a7-2a8b9811f369"}
