{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_015wpY1cJ5hPyqz9yRGigEbh","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t/*\n2\t * Copyright (c) 2009-2016 Petri Lehtinen <petri@digip.org>\n3\t * Copyright (c) 2011-2012 Basile Starynkevitch <basile@starynkevitch.net>\n4\t *\n5\t * Jansson is free software; you can redistribute it and/or modify it\n6\t * under the terms of the MIT license. See LICENSE for details.\n7\t */\n8\t\n9\t#include <stdlib.h>\n10\t#include <string.h>\n11\t\n12\t#include \"jansson.h\"\n13\t#include \"jansson_private.h\"\n14\t\n15\t/* C89 allows these to be macros */\n16\t#undef malloc\n17\t#undef realloc\n18\t#undef free\n19\t\n20\t/* memory function pointers */\n21\tstatic json_malloc_t do_malloc = malloc;\n22\tstatic json_realloc_t do_realloc = realloc;\n23\tstatic json_free_t do_free = free;\n24\t\n25\tvoid *jsonp_malloc(size_t size) {\n26\t    if (!size)\n27\t        return NULL;\n28\t\n29\t    return (*do_malloc)(size);\n30\t}\n31\t\n32\tvoid jsonp_free(void *ptr) {\n33\t    if (!ptr)\n34\t        return;\n35\t\n36\t    (*do_free)(ptr);\n37\t}\n38\t\n39\tvoid *jsonp_realloc(void *ptr, size_t originalSize, size_t newSize) {\n40\t    void *newMemory;\n41\t\n42\t    if (do_realloc)\n43\t        return (*do_realloc)(ptr, newSize);\n44\t\n45\t    // realloc emulation using malloc and free\n46\t    if (newSize == 0) {\n47\t        if (ptr != NULL)\n48\t            (*do_free)(ptr);\n49\t\n50\t        return NULL;\n51\t    } else {\n52\t        newMemory = (*do_malloc)(newSize);\n53\t\n54\t        if ((newMemory != NULL) && (ptr != NULL)) {\n55\t            memcpy(newMemory, ptr, (originalSize < newSize) ? originalSize : newSize);\n56\t\n57\t            (*do_free)(ptr);\n58\t        }\n59\t\n60\t        return newMemory;\n61\t    }\n62\t}\n63\t\n64\tchar *jsonp_strndup(const char *str, size_t len) {\n65\t    char *new_str;\n66\t\n67\t    new_str = jsonp_malloc(len + 1);\n68\t    if (!new_str)\n69\t        return NULL;\n70\t\n71\t    memcpy(new_str, str, len);\n72\t    new_str[len] = '\\0';\n73\t    return new_str;\n74\t}\n75\t\n76\tvoid json_set_alloc_funcs(json_malloc_t malloc_fn, json_free_t free_fn) {\n77\t    do_malloc = malloc_fn;\n78\t    do_realloc = NULL;\n79\t    do_free = free_fn;\n80\t}\n81\t\n82\tvoid json_set_alloc_funcs2(json_malloc_t malloc_fn, json_realloc_t realloc_fn,\n83\t                           json_free_t free_fn) {\n84\t    do_malloc = malloc_fn;\n85\t    do_realloc = realloc_fn;\n86\t    do_free = free_fn;\n87\t}\n88\t\n89\tvoid json_get_alloc_funcs(json_malloc_t *malloc_fn, json_free_t *free_fn) {\n90\t    if (malloc_fn)\n91\t        *malloc_fn = do_malloc;\n92\t    if (free_fn)\n93\t        *free_fn = do_free;\n94\t}\n95\tvoid json_get_alloc_funcs2(json_malloc_t *malloc_fn, json_realloc_t *realloc_fn,\n96\t                           json_free_t *free_fn) {\n97\t    if (malloc_fn)\n98\t        *malloc_fn = do_malloc;\n99\t    if (realloc_fn)\n100\t        *realloc_fn = do_realloc;\n101\t    if (free_fn)\n102\t        *free_fn = do_free;\n103\t}\n104\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01WcvaMYNMfrR18TTx6dMBBY","session_id":"0aaf00f6-473d-4ae1-b7a7-2a8b9811f369","uuid":"87b160fe-e3b6-4161-9e63-b880b98fbca5","timestamp":"2026-08-23T00:30:44.878Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write strbuffer differential tests"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_y6ijgxxywhf3xjohknp4amrscm4j7222krhrjmbimin47gd22bsq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01TfJ2ptRDhUxQLatYnis5yh","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Write hashtable differential tests","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"You are writing ONE Rust integration test file for a C-to-Rust differential verification project.\n\nWorking dir: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-hjHD91/translated_rust\n(always use absolute paths; the shell cwd is not reliable)\n\nREAD THESE FIRST (mandatory):\n- /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-hjHD91/translated_rust/tests/common/mod.rs   (harness)\n- /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-hjHD91/translated_rust/tests/t00_smoke.rs    (usage example)\n- /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-hjHD91/translated_rust/c_src/src/hashtable.c (C ground truth)\n- /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-hjHD91/translated_rust/c_src/src/hashtable.h\n- /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-hjHD91/translated_rust/c_src/src/lookup3.h   (the hash function)\n\nYOUR TASK: create exactly one new file\n  /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-hjHD91/translated_rust/tests/t03_hashtable.rs\n\nIt must start with:\n```rust\nmod common;\nuse common::*;\n```\n\n`duo()` returns `&'static Duo` with `.c` / `.rs` (`Lib`), each holding function\npointers named exactly like the C symbols. Already declared in the harness:\n```rust\nhashtable_init:         unsafe extern \"C\" fn(*mut hashtable_t) -> c_int,\nhashtable_close:        unsafe extern \"C\" fn(*mut hashtable_t),\nhashtable_set:          unsafe extern \"C\" fn(*mut hashtable_t, *const c_char, usize, *mut json_t) -> c_int,\nhashtable_get:          unsafe extern \"C\" fn(*mut hashtable_t, *const c_char, usize) -> *mut c_void,\nhashtable_del:          unsafe extern \"C\" fn(*mut hashtable_t, *const c_char, usize) -> c_int,\nhashtable_clear:        unsafe extern \"C\" fn(*mut hashtable_t),\nhashtable_iter:         unsafe extern \"C\" fn(*mut hashtable_t) -> *mut c_void,\nhashtable_iter_at:      unsafe extern \"C\" fn(*mut hashtable_t, *const c_char, usize) -> *mut c_void,\nhashtable_iter_next:    unsafe extern \"C\" fn(*mut hashtable_t, *mut c_void) -> *mut c_void,\nhashtable_iter_key:     unsafe extern \"C\" fn(*mut c_void) -> *mut c_char,\nhashtable_iter_key_len: unsafe extern \"C\" fn(*mut c_void) -> usize,\nhashtable_iter_value:   unsafe extern \"C\" fn(*mut c_void) -> *mut c_void,\nhashtable_iter_set:     unsafe extern \"C\" fn(*mut c_void, *mut json_t),\njson_integer:           unsafe extern \"C\" fn(i64) -> *mut json_t,\njson_string:            unsafe extern \"C\" fn(*const c_char) -> *mut json_t,\njson_null / json_true / json_false: unsafe extern \"C\" fn() -> *mut json_t,\njson_object_seed:       unsafe extern \"C\" fn(usize),\n```\nplus `hashtable_t` / `hashtable_list` (`#[repr(C)]`, with `hashtable_t::zeroed()`)\nand the `json_t { type_: c_int, refcount: usize }` struct.\n\nCRITICAL SETUP NOTES\n- `duo()` already calls `json_object_seed(TEST_SEED)` on BOTH libraries with the\n  same value, so `hashtable_seed` matches and bucket order is identical. Never\n  re-seed with a different value inside a test that compares iteration order —\n  `json_object_seed` is a no-op once the seed is non-zero anyway.\n- Values stored in a hashtable are `json_t*` that BELONG to that library\n  (`hashtable_set` steals the reference; `hashtable_del`/`clear`/`close` decref it).\n  So always build the C table's values with `d.c.json_integer(...)` and the Rust\n  table's with `d.rs.json_integer(...)`.\n- `hashtable_get` / `hashtable_iter_value` return the stored `json_t*`. Do NOT compare\n  raw pointers between libraries — compare the observable value, e.g.\n  `(l.json_integer_value)(p as *const json_t)`, and NULL-ness.\n- Always `hashtable_close` a table you initialised.\n\nWrite a snapshot helper that captures the full observable table state through the\npublic API only, e.g.\n```rust\n// -> (size, order, Vec<(key_bytes, integer_value)>) in iteration order\nfn snap(l: &Lib, ht: *mut hashtable_t) -> (usize, usize, Vec<(Vec<u8>, i64)>) { ... }\n```\nusing `hashtable_iter` / `hashtable_iter_next` / `hashtable_iter_key` /\n`hashtable_iter_key_len` / `hashtable_iter_value`, and compare C vs Rust with `eq(...)`.\nComparing the ITERATION ORDER is essential: it is hash- and bucket-dependent and is\nexactly where a translation bug would show up.\n\nWrite these tests, covering CONFIGS.md rows 12-22 and ERRORS.md rows 229-234:\n\n1. `init_state` — fresh table: return value, `size` (0), `order` (3, i.e.\n   INITIAL_HASHTABLE_ORDER). (CONFIGS 12)\n2. `set_counts_and_rehash` — insert N distinct keys and snapshot after EVERY insert,\n   for N = 1, 7, 8, 9, 100, 500. N = 8 triggers the first rehash (load ratio 1 with\n   8 buckets), 100 and 500 trigger several. Assert the `order` growth matches too.\n   (CONFIGS 13)\n3. `set_overwrite_existing` — set the same key repeatedly with different values;\n   `size` must stay 1 and the value must be replaced. Also interleave overwrites of\n   several keys among 50 entries. (CONFIGS 14)\n4. `key_shapes` — `key_len` 0 (empty key), 1, 2, 255, 1000; keys containing embedded\n   NUL bytes; two keys that share a prefix but differ in length (e.g. \"ab\" len 2 vs\n   \"ab\" len 1 taken from the same buffer) — the C compares `key_len` AND `memcmp`, so\n   these are DISTINCT keys. Snapshot after each. (CONFIGS 15; ERRORS 229)\n5. `get_present_and_absent` — for a 100-key table, `hashtable_get` every present key\n   and 100 absent keys; compare NULL-ness and the value. Also get with a `key_len`\n   one shorter / one longer than the stored key. (CONFIGS 16; ERRORS 229)\n6. `del_positions` — build tables of 1..12 entries and delete at every position\n   (first, middle, last), snapshotting after each delete; also delete an absent key\n   (must return -1 in both) and delete the same key twice. Additionally build a\n   100-key table and delete all keys in a seeded pseudo-random order, snapshotting\n   after every delete. (CONFIGS 17; ERRORS 230)\n7. `clear_and_reuse` — for 0, 1, 100 entries: `hashtable_clear`, snapshot\n   (`size` 0, `order` unchanged, empty iteration), then re-insert 20 keys and\n   snapshot. (CONFIGS 18)\n8. `iteration_full_traversal` — 0, 1, 8, 100 entries: full traversal comparing keys,\n   key lengths and values in order; `hashtable_iter` on an empty table must be NULL in\n   both; `hashtable_iter_next` from the last element must be NULL in both.\n   (CONFIGS 19; ERRORS 233,234)\n9. `iter_at_resume` — for a 60-key table, `hashtable_iter_at` at each key and then\n   traverse to the end, comparing the resulting key sequence. Also `hashtable_iter_at`\n   with an absent key (NULL in both) and with a wrong `key_len`.\n   (CONFIGS 20; ERRORS 232)\n10. `iter_set_replaces_value` — walk a 30-key table with iterators and\n    `hashtable_iter_set` a fresh value at every position; snapshot afterwards.\n    (CONFIGS 21)\n11. `randomized_operation_sequences` — 200 randomized sequences from a seeded `Rng`,\n    each performing 200 random operations chosen from {set new key, set existing key,\n    get, del existing, del absent, clear, full iterate}, snapshotting the whole table\n    after every operation. This is the most valuable test — make sure it really\n    compares full iteration order every step. (CONFIGS 13,14,16,17,19,22)\n12. `long_key_rejection` — `hashtable_set` with `key_len = usize::MAX` and\n    `key_len = usize::MAX - 8` must hit the `init_pair` overflow guard and return -1\n    in BOTH libraries without touching memory (pass a short but valid key pointer).\n    Compare the return value and the snapshot (unchanged). Note the C decides purely\n    from `key_len >= (size_t)-1 - offsetof(pair_t, key)`; compute a `key_len` just\n    below the threshold only if it would not actually attempt 