{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01K259voAET1iQ8RNgQ2btWt","type":"tool_result","content":"############ FIX 1: src/pack_unpack.rs set_error! ############\n/* set_error(): C's version passes the caller's format string and arguments\n   STRAIGHT THROUGH to jsonp_error_vset(), which runs vsnprintf() directly on\n   error->text:\n\n       static void set_error(scanner_t *s, const char *source,\n                             enum json_error_code code, const char *fmt, ...) {\n           va_start(ap, fmt);\n           jsonp_error_vset(s->error, s->token.line, s->token.column,\n                            s->token.pos, code, fmt, ap);\n           jsonp_error_set_source(s->error, source);\n           va_end(ap);\n       }\n\n   Formatting into a scratch buffer first and re-emitting it with \"%s\" is NOT\n   equivalent: several messages here use \"%c\", and when the character is '\\0'\n   (an unexpected format character at the end of the format string) the direct\n   vsnprintf still writes the bytes that follow the embedded NUL, while a \"%s\"\n   pass would stop there. So inline jsonp_error_vset()'s body and let snprintf\n   write into error->text itself. jsonp_error_set_source() is called\n   unconditionally, exactly as in the C. */\nmacro_rules! set_error {\n    ($s:expr, $source:expr, $code:expr, $fmt:expr $(, $arg:expr)*) => {{\n        let __s = $s;\n        let __e: *mut json_error_t = (*__s).error;\n        /* jsonp_error_vset(): no-op on NULL, and never overwrites */\n        if !__e.is_null() && (*__e).text[0] == 0 {\n            (*__e).line = (*__s).token.line;\n            (*__e).column = (*__s).token.column;\n            (*__e).position = (*__s).token.pos as c_int;\n            libc::snprintf(\n                (*__e).text.as_mut_ptr(),\n                JSON_ERROR_TEXT_LENGTH - 1,\n                $fmt.as_ptr() as *const c_char\n                $(, $arg)*\n            );\n            (*__e).text[JSON_ERROR_TEXT_LENGTH - 2] = 0;\n            (*__e).text[JSON_ERROR_TEXT_LENGTH - 1] = $code as c_char;\n        }\n        jsonp_error_set_source((*__s).error, $source.as_ptr() as *const c_char);\n    }};\n}\n\n############ FIX 2: src/strconv.rs jsonp_strtod assert ############\n\n/* c_src/src/strconv.c is compiled WITHOUT -DNDEBUG, so its\n *     assert(end == strbuffer->value + strbuffer->length);\n * is live: a caller passing a strbuffer that strtod() does not consume in full\n * makes the C library call __assert_fail() and abort. jsonp_strtod is an\n * exported symbol, so this is reachable from outside and has to be reproduced\n * here, otherwise the Rust would silently return 0 where the C aborts. */\nconst ASSERT_MSG_STRTOD: &[u8] = b\"end == strbuffer->value + strbuffer->length\\0\";\nconst ASSERT_FILE_STRTOD: &[u8] =\n    concat!(env!(\"CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR\"), \"/c_src/src/strconv.c\\0\").as_bytes();\nconst ASSERT_FUNC_STRTOD: &[u8] = b\"jsonp_strtod\\0\";\n\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\npub unsafe extern \"C\" fn jsonp_strtod(strbuffer: *mut strbuffer_t, out: *mut f64) -> c_int {\n    let value: f64;\n    let mut end: *mut c_char = std::ptr::null_mut();\n\n    to_locale(strbuffer);\n\n    libc::set_errno(0);\n    value = libc::strtod((*strbuffer).value, &mut end);\n\n    /* assert(end == strbuffer->value + strbuffer->length); */\n    if end != (*strbuffer).value.add((*strbuffer).length) {\n        libc::__assert_fail(\n            ASSERT_MSG_STRTOD.as_ptr() as *const c_char,\n            ASSERT_FILE_STRTOD.as_ptr() as *const c_char,\n            53,\n            ASSERT_FUNC_STRTOD.as_ptr() as *const c_char,\n        );\n    }\n\n    if (value == libc::HUGE_VAL || value == -libc::HUGE_VAL) && libc::errno() == libc::ERANGE {\n        /* Overflow */\n        return -1;\n    }\n\n    *out = value;\n    0\n}\n\n\n############ FIX 2b: src/libc.rs __assert_fail ############\n105-    /// glibc's assertion-failure hook, so live `assert()`s in the C sources\n106-    /// abort with the same message here.\n107:    pub fn __assert_fail(\n108-        assertion: *const c_char,\n109-        file: *const c_char,\n110-        line: u32,\n111-        function: *const c_char,\n112-    ) -> !;\n113-\n114-    pub static stdin: *mut FILE;","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0aaf00f6-473d-4ae1-b7a7-2a8b9811f369","uuid":"d56cb554-fcb4-4d6d-9b04-9fd19ae28d4b","timestamp":"2026-08-23T01:49:45.431Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"############ FIX 1: src/pack_unpack.rs set_error! ############\n/* set_error(): C's version passes the caller's format string and arguments\n   STRAIGHT THROUGH to jsonp_error_vset(), which runs vsnprintf() directly on\n   error->text:\n\n       static void set_error(scanner_t *s, const char *source,\n                             enum json_error_code code, const char *fmt, ...) {\n           va_start(ap, fmt);\n           jsonp_error_vset(s->error, s->token.line, s->token.column,\n                            s->token.pos, code, fmt, ap);\n           jsonp_error_set_source(s->error, source);\n           va_end(ap);\n       }\n\n   Formatting into a scratch buffer first and re-emitting it with \"%s\" is NOT\n   equivalent: several messages here use \"%c\", and when the character is '\\0'\n   (an unexpected format character at the end of the format string) the direct\n   vsnprintf still writes the bytes that follow the embedded NUL, while a \"%s\"\n   pass would stop there. So inline jsonp_error_vset()'s body and let snprintf\n   write into error->text itself. jsonp_error_set_source() is called\n   unconditionally, exactly as in the C. */\nmacro_rules! set_error {\n    ($s:expr, $source:expr, $code:expr, $fmt:expr $(, $arg:expr)*) => {{\n        let __s = $s;\n        let __e: *mut json_error_t = (*__s).error;\n        /* jsonp_error_vset(): no-op on NULL, and never overwrites */\n        if !__e.is_null() && (*__e).text[0] == 0 {\n            (*__e).line = (*__s).token.line;\n            (*__e).column = (*__s).token.column;\n            (*__e).position = (*__s).token.pos as c_int;\n            libc::snprintf(\n                (*__e).text.as_mut_ptr(),\n                JSON_ERROR_TEXT_LENGTH - 1,\n                $fmt.as_ptr() as *const c_char\n                $(, $arg)*\n            );\n            (*__e).text[JSON_ERROR_TEXT_LENGTH - 2] = 0;\n            (*__e).text[JSON_ERROR_TEXT_LENGTH - 1] = $code as c_char;\n        }\n        jsonp_error_set_source((*__s).error, $source.as_ptr() as *const c_char);\n    }};\n}\n\n############ FIX 2: src/strconv.rs jsonp_strtod assert ############\n\n/* c_src/src/strconv.c is compiled WITHOUT -DNDEBUG, so its\n *     assert(end == strbuffer->value + strbuffer->length);\n * is live: a caller passing a strbuffer that strtod() does not consume in full\n * makes the C library call __assert_fail() and abort. jsonp_strtod is an\n * exported symbol, so this is reachable from outside and has to be reproduced\n * here, otherwise the Rust would silently return 0 where the C aborts. */\nconst ASSERT_MSG_STRTOD: &[u8] = b\"end == strbuffer->value + strbuffer->length\\0\";\nconst ASSERT_FILE_STRTOD: &[u8] =\n    concat!(env!(\"CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR\"), \"/c_src/src/strconv.c\\0\").as_bytes();\nconst ASSERT_FUNC_STRTOD: &[u8] = b\"jsonp_strtod\\0\";\n\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\npub unsafe extern \"C\" fn jsonp_strtod(strbuffer: *mut strbuffer_t, out: *mut f64) -> c_int {\n    let value: f64;\n    let mut end: *mut c_char = std::ptr::null_mut();\n\n    to_locale(strbuffer);\n\n    libc::set_errno(0);\n    value = libc::strtod((*strbuffer).value, &mut end);\n\n    /* assert(end == strbuffer->value + strbuffer->length); */\n    if end != (*strbuffer).value.add((*strbuffer).length) {\n        libc::__assert_fail(\n            ASSERT_MSG_STRTOD.as_ptr() as *const c_char,\n            ASSERT_FILE_STRTOD.as_ptr() as *const c_char,\n            53,\n            ASSERT_FUNC_STRTOD.as_ptr() as *const c_char,\n        );\n    }\n\n    if (value == libc::HUGE_VAL || value == -libc::HUGE_VAL) && libc::errno() == libc::ERANGE {\n        /* Overflow */\n        return -1;\n    }\n\n    *out = value;\n    0\n}\n\n\n############ FIX 2b: src/libc.rs __assert_fail ############\n105-    /// glibc's assertion-failure hook, so live `assert()`s in the C sources\n106-    /// abort with the same message here.\n107:    pub fn __assert_fail(\n108-        assertion: *const c_char,\n109-        file: *const c_char,\n110-        line: u32,\n111-        function: *const c_char,\n112-    ) -> !;\n113-\n114-    pub static stdin: *mut FILE;","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_qeutsgwqqgtb7tvvd5zalutuztmujlwsxpdatgeic3hl6ffm2vyq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. Here's the summary.\n\n## Result\n\n**Two real C↔Rust divergences found and fixed in the Rust** (the C was never touched):\n\n1. **`pack_unpack.c` `set_error()`** — the C passes the caller's format string straight through to `jsonp_error_vset()`, which runs `vsnprintf()` **directly on `error->text`**. The Rust formatted into a scratch buffer and re-emitted it via `\"%s\"`. Not equivalent: these messages use `\"%c\"`, and when the unexpected format character is `'\\0'` (format string ran out), the direct `vsnprintf` still writes the bytes *after* the embedded NUL — C left `text[30] == '\\''`, Rust left `text[30] == 0`. Fixed by inlining `jsonp_error_vset`'s body into `set_error!`. (`load.c` genuinely *does* use the `\"%s\"` indirection, so `src/load.rs` was already correct and left alone.)\n\n2. **`strconv.c:53`'s live `assert()`** — CMake defines no `NDEBUG`, and `jsonp_strtod` is an exported symbol, so a strbuffer that `strtod` doesn't fully consume (`\"1e\"`, `\"zzz\"`, `\"1.5abc\"`) makes the C `__assert_fail()` and abort while the Rust silently returned `0`. Fixed via glibc `__assert_fail` with the same assertion/file/function; `t10_abort_parity.rs` re-execs itself in a child and asserts both die on SIGABRT with the same message.\n\n## Completion gate\n\n- **SYMBOLS.md** — 130/130 exported symbols match exactly, **0 missing, 0 extra**, for both the debug *and* release `.so`; all undefined symbols are libc/unwind imports.\n- **CONFIGS.md** — **138/138** rows pass, each driven through both `.so`s with randomized inputs at fixed seeds.\n- **ERRORS.md** — **258/258** rows pass, each asserting the identical sentinel *and* all 252 raw bytes of `json_error_t`.\n- **Feature combinations** — `Cargo.toml` declares no `[features]` and no optional deps (`cargo metadata` → `features: {}`), so there is exactly one combination. All 131 tests pass under `cargo test`, `cargo test --no-default-features`, and `cargo test --release`.\n\n## Two things worth flagging\n\n- I validated the suite isn't vacuous by **mutation testing** — 9 single-line defects injected into `src/` were each caught (table in ERRORS.md). Notably, an array-capacity change is invisible to the value API but is caught by `t09_misc`'s allocation-call-sequence comparison. One mutation (`compare_keys` `min→max`) was *not* caught, and I verified it is genuinely behaviour-equivalent for NUL-terminated keys rather than a test gap.\n- Two apparent divergences in `dumps_real_precision` turned out to be **races in `dtoa.c` itself** (compiled without `MULTIPLE_THREADS`, so `Balloc`'s freelist, `p5s` and `dtoa_result` are unsynchronized statics in *both* libraries). Tests that format reals now take a process-wide mutex; this is a C-library property, not a translation defect.\n\nOne process note: a 10-minute tool timeout killed a mutation run mid-flight and left `src/utf.rs` mutated. I caught it, restored it from backup, and re-confirmed every source file is clean and all suites pass — but a cleaner runner (trap-based restore) would have avoided the risk."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2603,"cache_read_input_tokens":473627,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2603,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0aaf00f6-473d-4ae1-b7a7-2a8b9811f369","uuid":"d94f0743-7bb7-4ad5-a0f7-af9713ccae15","timestamp":"2026-08-23T01:50:02.693Z"}
